{:event/id "472199eb-dd22-4fe6-a810-c4a4f68cfb03" :event/schema {:schema/root "8eb19ab0e4b70e205a84a627fd3e15c3ba79c0fd8c792d37e72f3c79511e0bf9" :schema/id :archaeology/finding-recorded :schema/hash "f2ad682fa2ac851a5d18b436849d3c59f07eb5f9ebe6179d231d7254b8d89848"} :event/type :archaeology/finding-recorded :event/stream "archaeology:findings:366ba6e8-d943-486c-921f-a2b30e926ed6" :event/seq 2 :event/causes ["4f4732e3-1969-4f30-b83e-74f0f42ff3bb"] :event/actor "agent:chatgpt" :event/subject "archaeology:366ba6e8-d943-486c-921f-a2b30e926ed6" :event/at "2026-08-30T13:49:37Z" :event/data {:finding/id :knoxx/reservation-state-shared-recovery-policy-not :finding/shapes #{:state-machine :protocol :idempotency} :finding/current-name "IIdempotencyStore + ITranslationEvidenceStore/reserve-dispatch!" :finding/candidate-name "ReservationGate<Key,Claim>" :finding/claim "Publication effects and translation dispatch both require an atomic claim before a side effect and expose reserved, in-flight, and done outcomes. That claim-state law is common. Recovery is not: a failed external publication is ambiguous and leaves the reservation in flight until the target is observed, while an in-process agent dispatch throw proves no trigger ran and is conclusively retriable; a dead agent session has no observer and needs an operator. A shared idempotent-executor abstraction would erase these domain-specific recovery laws."}}
